### Summary

`surrealdb-parser` (workspace crate `surrealdb/parser` in `surrealdb/surrealdb`) is the in-repo next-generation SurrealQL parser. Its lexer wrapper `PeekableLexer<'source, 4>` keeps lookahead tokens in `[MaybeUninit<Result<Token, LexError>>; 4]` with two `u8` cursors, and `Parser::peek1()`/`peek2()` expose one- and two-token lookahead through the public, safe `Parse` trait (`async fn parse(parser: &mut Parser<'_, '_>) -> ParseResult<Self>`). In `surrealdb/parser/src/peekable.rs:88-103` the loop that is meant to lex forward to the requested offset is inverted: the inclusive range `write..=read` is empty whenever the target is ahead of the buffered count, so any forward jump (`OFFSET > buffered`) skips lexing and then executes `self.peek[read as usize].assume_init()` on a slot that was never written. Because `Token` contains the fieldless enum `BaseTokenKind`, constructing `Result<Token, LexError>` from uninitialized bytes reads an uninitialized enum discriminant - undefined behavior. Miri confirms it at `surrealdb/parser/src/peekable.rs:103:17`: `constructing invalid value of type std::result::Result<surrealdb_token::Token, surrealdb_token::LexError>: at .<enum-tag>, encountered uninitialized memory, but expected an integer`. A second, deterministic consequence (no UB required) is that consumed ring slots are never cleared, so `peek1()` can return a stale, already-consumed token; the inlined integration test shows this under a plain `cargo test` (it returns the token at span `0..1` after the three-token input `"a b c"` has been fully consumed; the correct result is `None`). No `unsafe` is needed at the call site: any crate implementing the public, unsealed `Parse` trait receives a `&mut Parser` and can call `peek1()`/`peek2()` before peeking the current token (or skip an offset), then run `Parser::enter_parse::<P>(...)`.

### Details

**Verified snapshot.** `surrealdb 3.3.0-nightly`, git commit `18971ffba781a796c0ad3897bb4b82432c241e4c` (2026-09-04); file `surrealdb/parser/src/peekable.rs` (166 lines), package `surrealdb-parser` (`surrealdb/parser/Cargo.toml:2`), workspace member at `Cargo.toml:13`. The crate is workspace-internal: `publish.workspace = true` and the workspace sets `publish = false` (`Cargo.toml:35`), so it is not on crates.io; the workspace alias is declared at `Cargo.toml:60`. Upstream `surrealdb/surrealdb` `main` carries the identical `peek::<OFFSET>` body (verified 2026-09-21), so this is not a fork-local defect. We verified only this snapshot; please set the final affected/patched ranges when publishing.

**Root cause.** `PeekableLexer` (`peekable.rs:8-13`) initialises every slot with `MaybeUninit::uninit()` (`:37`). `peek::<OFFSET>` (`:64-105`) handles `OFFSET == 0` by lexing when the buffer is empty; for `OFFSET > 0` it computes the target ring index and then tries to fill up to it:

```rust
// surrealdb/parser/src/peekable.rs:87-104 (as verified)
} else {
    let write = self.write & Self::MASK;
    let read = self.read.wrapping_add(OFFSET) & Self::MASK;

    let write = if write < read {
        write + SIZE as u8
    } else {
        write
    };

    for _ in write..=read {
        let x = self.lex_token()?;
        self.peek[(self.write & Self::MASK) as usize] = MaybeUninit::new(x);
        self.write = self.write.wrapping_add(1);
    }

    Some(unsafe { self.peek[read as usize].assume_init() })
}
```

The range adjustment is backwards. When the target is ahead of the write cursor in ring order (`write < read`), `write` is pushed past `read` by `SIZE`, so the inclusive range `write..=read` is empty; when the target is behind (`write > read`), the range is also empty; only `write == read` runs the body, lexing exactly one token. The only case that fills anything is therefore "the requested offset is exactly the next unbuffered position", i.e. the sequential `peek()` → `peek1()` → `peek2()` pattern used by the crate's own call sites. The intent was clear...

# Cross-field array-index drift in wildcard field-permission filtering

## Summary

At revision `18971ffba781a796c0ad3897bb4b82432c241e4c`, `filter_fields_by_permission` can disclose a child field that its `SELECT` permission denied when an earlier overlapping wildcard permission removes array elements. The function expands every permission path from one immutable pre-filter snapshot but applies each denial immediately to a mutable result. An authenticated low-privilege record user can therefore receive a denied value from a surviving element after an ancestor permission compacts the array and invalidates the concrete indices used by a later child permission.

## Technical Details

The affected function is `surrealdb_core::exec::operators::scan::pipeline::filter_fields_by_permission` in `surrealdb/core/src/exec/operators/scan/pipeline.rs`, lines 942-990 at the tested revision. The shipped query path is `Datastore::execute` (`surrealdb/core/src/kvs/ds.rs`, lines 3437-3468), `process_plan_inner` (`ds.rs`, lines 3870-3918), `Executor::execute_plan` (`surrealdb/core/src/dbs/executor.rs`, lines 1688-1718), `filter_and_process_batch` (`pipeline.rs`, lines 390-450), and then `filter_fields_by_permission`. `filter_and_process_batch` invokes the affected function before evaluating the `WHERE` predicate when permission checking is enabled.

The implementation deliberately snapshots the row once so that predicates and wildcard expansion observe the original document, while denied paths are removed immediately from the mutable result:

```rust
let mut snapshot: Option<Value> = None;
for (idiom, perm) in state.field_permissions.iter() {
    // ...
    PhysicalPermission::Conditional(_) => {
        let original = snapshot.get_or_insert_with(|| value.clone());
        for path in original.each(&idiom.0).into_iter().rev() {
            let field_value = original.pick(&path.0);
            let allowed =
                check_permission_for_value(perm, original, Some(&field_value), ctx).await?;
            if !allowed {
                value.cut(&path.0);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Reverse iteration preserves indices while processing multiple removals for one wildcard field. It does not preserve element identity across different overlapping fields. If the ordered permission entries are `items[*]` followed by `items[*].secret`, the first entry can compact `value.items`, while the second entry still expands, picks, and authorizes paths against the original `snapshot.items`. The later concrete path is then cut from the already-compacted mutable array.

The decisive input contains four elements: `[{n:0,secret:"s0"},{n:1,secret:"s1"},{n:2,secret:"s2"},{n:3,secret:"s3"}]`. The parent permission retains only even `n`, producing mutable elements `n=0` and `n=2`. The child permission permits only the original value `s1`. When the child loop reaches original index 1, it sees allowed value `s1` and does not cut mutable index 1, but mutable index 1 is now the `n=2` element containing denied value `s2`. The result is `[(0, None), (2, Some("s2"))]`; the required result is `[(0, None), (2, None)]`.

The violated invariant is: every denied concrete field value must be removed from a record-user `SELECT` result while preserving the identity of each array element across overlapping field permissions. This is unintended authorization behavior because the permission boundary promises to remove denied fields before returning the row, and the repository's regression coverage for issue #7356 explicitly requires array-element permissions to filter elements by identity.

Preconditions are an authenticated record user, a readable row containing an array, two overlapping wildcard field permissions processed parent first and child second, and a parent predicate that removes enough elements to shift a survivor into an index whose original child value was allowed. No administrator capability, malformed input, race, or memory corruption is required.

